In modern full-stack web applications, TypeScript's type checker operates exclusively at compile time. At runtime, the browser receives raw, untyped strings over HTTP.

The Illusion of Type Assertions (`as Type`):
A dangerous and widespread junior pattern is casting untrusted API responses directly:
`const data = (await res.json()) as ModelResponse;`
If the API returns a 502 Bad Gateway HTML error page, an unexpected null field, or a breaking schema change, TypeScript remains completely silent at compile time. The application crashes at runtime with `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')`.

Discriminated Unions for Clean UI States:
Avoid maintaining independent, uncoordinated boolean flags (`isLoading`, `isError`, `data`, `error`), which permit illegal states (e.g. `isLoading: true` and `isError: true` at the same time). Instead, model the UI state machine as a Discriminated Union:
```typescript
type UIState =
  | { status: 'idle' }
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'streaming'; text: string; tokenCount: number }
  | { status: 'success'; result: DocumentResult }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string; retryable: boolean };
```
Runtime Narrowing with Schema Validation:
To safely bridge runtime JSON to compile-time TypeScript types, use runtime validation libraries like Zod or custom Type Guards:
```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const DocumentResultSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().uuid(),
  title: z.string().min(1),
  score: z.number().min(0).max(1),
  tags: z.array(z.string())
});
type DocumentResult = z.infer<typeof DocumentResultSchema>;

const parsed = DocumentResultSchema.safeParse(rawJson);
if (!parsed.success) {
  setUIState({ status: 'error', message: 'Schema mismatch', retryable: false });
  return;
}
setUIState({ status: 'success', result: parsed.data });
```
In the UI rendering layer, an exhaustive `switch (state.status)` block ensures that every state variant is handled cleanly. If a new status is added to the union, TypeScript's `default: const _exhaustive: never = state;` throws a compile-time error until the developer adds the missing UI view.

example.code:
import { z } from 'zod';

const ApiResponseSchema = z.discriminatedUnion('type', [
  z.object({ type: z.literal('token'), text: z.string() }),
  z.object({ type: z.literal('done'), total_tokens: z.number() }),
  z.object({ type: z.literal('error'), code: z.string(), message: z.string() })
]);

export type StreamMessage = z.infer<typeof ApiResponseSchema>;

export function handleMessage(raw: unknown) {
  const result = ApiResponseSchema.safeParse(raw);
  if (!result.success) {
    console.error('Invalid message format:', result.error.format());
    return;
  }
  const msg = result.data;
  switch (msg.type) {
    case 'token':
      console.log('Appended token:', msg.text);
      break;
    case 'done':
      console.log('Finished with tokens:', msg.total_tokens);
      break;
    case 'error':
      console.error('Error received:', msg.message);
      break;
  }
}

